        "source_run_id": "r001-retrospective",
        "parent_request_id": "r001-retrospective",
        "objective": "Proposed EXECUTION-PROMPT v2 change",
        "text": "0a1,2\n> EXECUTION-PROMPT v2 (2026-10-09). Changes from v1: section 19A added from R001 evidence. v1 sha256 e1b5ca33c7f1d85c5b8b2145be2133ec50e811feaf13e9a97bc3a5fcda5439d6.\n> \n836a839,851\n> \n> \n> 19A. OPERATING LESSONS (added in prompt v2 from R001 evidence)\n> \n> These instructions refine how earlier sections are carried out. They add no authority, remove no gate, and do not change budgets. v2 takes effect at the R002 run boundary; R001 is assessed under v1. Trigger evidence: MISSION-JOURNAL.md entries cited by date.\n> \n> - Owner authorizations reach workers verbatim. Only an explicit, dated instruction from the user that is recorded in the journal may be passed on: quote it with its date and scope in the goal and as a --constraint. Never infer, paraphrase or broaden an authorization; it never overrides higher-priority instructions or the pause-and-help rule; if its scope is unclear, pause under section 17. (Trigger: journal 2026-10-09T09:35Z, a worker refused an approved data fetch because the repository README documented a restriction.)\n> - Third-party data is checked before any public push. Before the first public push of fetched data or evidence, read the source's terms and record the URL, date and conclusion in the run records. Keep bulk responses local; publish aggregates and at most 5 short attributed example records per table. Before each push, run a scan as its own step that counts records copied from source responses in every staged file; any staged file with more than 25 such records blocks the push until it is reviewed. If the terms are absent, ambiguous or forbid publication, or bulk data has already reached a public branch, pause under section 17 and ask before any history rewrite. (Trigger: journal 2026-10-09T04:45Z.)\n> - Dream budgets are sized before launch within the already approved budget: set max_calls to at least clamp(max_candidates // 2, 1, 3) + max_candidates, or reduce max_candidates; if that would exceed the approved budget, pause and ask rather than raise it. Record the number. (Trigger: journal 2026-10-08T14:58Z, pass 1 ended PARTIAL.)\n> - A Howl repair is reviewed against the repository's required regression gate: pass that gate as --verify and set --verify-timeout to at least twice its last observed runtime, recording both. Before merge, a committed candidate may be proven through the public `howl` CLI with PYTHONPATH pointing at a clean repair worktree (record the SHA in the journal); this supplements and never replaces independent review, acceptance, the section 16 merge gate, or post-merge public proof. Never switch the shared engine checkout. (Trigger: journal 2026-10-08T17:45Z, DOG-036.)\n> - After every HowlPlane session, check `howl agents doctor` for readiness changes and trace any downgrade to its cause before relying on it. Recover only through documented, non-manual operations (for example the documented live doctor); if the cause is unknown or recovery would require editing runtime state, pause under section 17. (Triggers: CUBS-P-002 at 17:45Z on 2026-10-08, DOG-039 at 23:35Z.)\n> - One deliverable stage per session: keep data acquisition and evaluation in separate goals. A second session on a worktree with an unfinished one needs a distinct Git worktree (`--separate`); otherwise discard only a mission-owned session, and only after preserving its uncommitted work as a recorded patch or archive. (Triggers: journal 2026-10-09T09:35Z S2 block; NOTE-008 at 15:35Z.)\n> - After a rejected acceptance, the same acceptor judges again (pin it with --orchestrator) with its quoted reasons in the goal, within the existing rework bounds; never route a rejection to a different acceptor. If that acceptor is unavailable or conflicted, pause under section 17. (Trigger: journal 2026-10-09T15:20Z S3 and S3b.)\n",
        "condition": "dream",
